Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the fire-ready Next.js project.

## Changes Made:

### 1. Installed @vercel/analytics package
- Added `@vercel/analytics` version 2.0.1 to dependencies
- Updated package.json and yarn.lock using Yarn package manager

### 2. Configured Analytics component in Next.js App Router
- Modified `src/app/layout.tsx` to import the Analytics component from `@vercel/analytics/next`
- Added `<Analytics />` component before the closing `</body>` tag in the RootLayout
- Organized imports according to project's Biome linter rules (alphabetically within groups)

### 3. Verified implementation
- Successfully built the project with `yarn build` - no errors
- Passed linter checks with `yarn lint` - no issues
- All existing tests pass with `yarn test` (39 tests passed)

## Implementation Details:

Following the official Vercel Analytics documentation (fetched from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), I implemented the Next.js App Router pattern:

1. Imported the Analytics component: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
2. Placed the component inside the `<body>` tag but outside the AppRouterCacheProvider to ensure it tracks all page views
3. The Analytics component is now positioned after all the main content to avoid blocking rendering

## Files Modified:

- `package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `src/app/layout.tsx` - Added Analytics import and component
- `yarn.lock` - Updated with new dependency (automatically by Yarn)

## Next Steps:

To activate analytics:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard for this project
2. Deploy the application to Vercel
3. Verify analytics are being tracked by checking the browser's Network tab for requests to `/<unique-path>/view`

The implementation is complete and ready for deployment.
